    "findings": [
      "All six authenticated Linux logs show agent 20261111.635, Ubuntu 26.04 and an exact hit on the same 1594353071-byte Cargo archive. Five used image 20261116.202.1. The two baseline observations on that image span 546 to 706 seconds; the physical host and load are unobserved. The retained 625-second baseline remains the authoritative three-run window.",
      "Median queue, setup, harness, tail and runner shifted from 3, 79, 513, 6 and 622 seconds to 2, 77, 589, 6 and 679 seconds. Restore improved from 49 to 44 seconds. The check-work sums count concurrent Python and Rust work and cannot be used as elapsed wall time.",
      "The same six local Rust packages rebuilt once per suite across the workspace and portable library commands. Cargo workspace test preparation rose from 30.35 to 38.07 seconds at the median. No Cargo lock waits were recorded in those logs. Operations reused the test build and the two recovery fixtures reused the compiled history bridge with at most 0.12 seconds per Cargo invocation.",
      "Python unit counts varied from 696–700 to 705–714 and overlapped the initial Rust lane. Workspace-timeframe ran 56, 56 and 58 cases in the controls and 58 in each new run, with two workers. The largest measured browser increases were distributed across workspace-timeframe and several market checks.",
      "No removable repeated build or other attributable corrective revision was identified. The new window uses three different ordinary PR heads with the same base and passes the growth tolerance. It establishes the current measurement. Task recovery still requires a reproduced cost, an exact repair and a three-run comparable post-repair window."
